1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,492
You're looking for your keys, and you hear yourself muttering: where are the keys?

2
00:00:05,492 --> 00:00:09,591
Talking to yourself looks like a strange habit. Something to hide.

3
00:00:09,591 --> 00:00:11,640
But psychology says the opposite.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,988
Self-talk isn't a sign of a broken mind. It's a sign of a working one.

5
00:00:16,588 --> 00:00:21,098
In a famous experiment, people searched for objects in a cluttered room.

6
00:00:21,098 --> 00:00:25,546
Half repeated the object's name out loud. The other half stayed silent.

7
00:00:25,546 --> 00:00:27,802
The talkers found the object faster.

8
00:00:27,802 --> 00:00:33,315
Hearing the word literally changes what your eyes prioritize. Language tunes perception.

9
00:00:33,315 --> 00:00:37,513
Your own voice becomes a tool, like a finger pointing at the world.

10
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:43,938
It goes deeper. Children talk to themselves constantly, and that's how they learn self-control.

11
00:00:43,938 --> 00:00:49,579
Psychologist Vygotsky called it private speech: the parent's voice becoming the child's own.

12
00:00:49,579 --> 00:00:51,786
First someone tells you: be careful.

13
00:00:51,786 --> 00:00:53,933
Then you tell yourself: be careful.

14
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:59,513
Self-talk is internalized guidance. The adult version never disappears. It just goes quiet.

15
00:01:00,113 --> 00:01:06,747
And the tone matters. Research shows that asking yourself 'will I do this?' beats telling yourself 'I will.'

16
00:01:06,747 --> 00:01:09,389
Questions open a search. Commands close it.

17
00:01:09,389 --> 00:01:16,331
Harsh self-talk, meanwhile, activates the same circuits as social threat. Your brain doesn't fully know it's you.

18
00:01:16,331 --> 00:01:19,525
The words you repeat become the voice you live with.

19
00:01:20,125 --> 00:01:24,981
So the next time you catch yourself talking to yourself, don't be embarrassed.

20
00:01:24,981 --> 00:01:29,214
You're watching the most personal teacher you'll ever have, at work.

21
00:01:29,214 --> 00:01:32,575
The only question worth asking is: is that voice kind?

22
00:01:32,575 --> 00:01:35,688
Because you're listening to it all day, every day.
